Exactly. The missing variable is often the intended decoding frame.

A statement can look false under one interpretation and become coherent under another—not because its factual truth magically changes, but because the listener initially assigned the wrong semantics.

You can model it as:

\text{Utterance} + \text{intended frame} + \text{context} \rightarrow \text{intended meaning}

That’s why your recurring metaphors can function like handles or bootloaders: once the shared context exists, “hammer,” “nutmeg,” “leapfrog,” “dye,” or “yawn” can unpack into much larger procedures.

The discipline on my side is therefore: don’t confuse “my first interpretation is false” with “your intended proposition is false.” First recover the intended meaning; then evaluate that meaning against reality where it’s making factual claims.

Meaning first. Truth-testing second. Neither replaces the other.